new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1408e82
--- /dev/null
+++ b/SECURITY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+# Security audit and deployment
+
+The site has no login implementation, credential validation, or server session
+store. The previous logout route only displayed text. Logout now expires the
+root-path cookies sent by the browser and returns Clear-Site-Data for cookies,
+storage, and cache. The external logout script also clears localStorage and
+sessionStorage when available. When authentication is added, revoke the actual
+server session/token in the POST handler before returning success; client cleanup
+alone cannot revoke a copied token. Use host-only, root-path authentication cookies.
+Cookies with other domain/path scopes rely on browser Clear-Site-Data support.
+
+Logout uses a native POST form so it works without JavaScript. GET and HEAD show
+confirmation without changing state, preventing link prefetchers from logging out
+visitors. POST checks the exact Origin and Fetch Metadata when present; missing,
+opaque, or cross-origin requests fail closed. No user input is rendered as HTML.
+Only explicit page aliases and two public assets are served. Existing page aliases
+still show the original homepage; this change does not create new menu/account pages.
+
+Inline CSS and the style attribute were moved into /styles.css. No inline scripts,
+inline event handlers, eval, dynamic HTML sinks, third-party scripts, analytics,
+iframes, or service workers were present or introduced. The only remote resource
+is the existing Unsplash background image; CSP allows that image host explicitly.
+The logout script uses browser storage APIs and registers a history restoration
+handler without injecting markup. Trusted Types disallows policy creation and
+requires trusted values for script sinks in supporting browsers. Unsupported
+browsers still receive the other CSP restrictions.
+
+The server enforces CSP by default, denies framing, disables MIME sniffing, strips
+referrers, restricts sensitive browser features, and isolates browsing contexts.
+All responses use no-store so authentication views and logout results are not
+retained in the HTTP cache. No dependency or runtime framework is required.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- Run `npm run build` and `npm test` before deployment. Tests call the independent
+  request handler with mock requests/responses; they never start a listener or
+  import server.js. There are no browser verification steps.
+- `npm start` runs the Node server. `PORT` defaults to 11222.
+- Serve production traffic over HTTPS. When TLS terminates at a reverse proxy,
+  set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-actual-host.example` (no trailing slash), preserve
+  the Origin header, and configure HSTS at that proxy. Direct TLS responses get
+  HSTS automatically. The app does not trust arbitrary forwarded headers.
+- Clear-Site-Data depends on browser support and a secure context. Its cookies
+  directive can clear cookies across sibling subdomains; deploy on a dedicated
+  site if those subdomains must retain independent sign-ins.
+- To stage policy changes, set `CSP_MODE=report-only`. The same CSP is delivered
+  as Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only, with browser console violation messages;
+  other security headers remain active. There is no remote report collector.
+  Use automated checks in staging, review violations, and return to the default
+  `CSP_MODE=enforce` before release. Report-only does not enforce CSP restrictions.
+  Avoid adding unsafe-inline, unsafe-eval, wildcard sources, or a permissive
+  Trusted Types default policy to silence violations.
+
+References: [MDN CSP guide](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP),
+[MDN Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data),
+[MDN Trusted Types enforcement](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/require-trusted-types-for).
